WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:11.600
Today we are going to learn how to take an object&nbsp;
in the real world, make a digital copy of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.960
and place it into our virtual world.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:21.440
This can be done through something that we&nbsp;
call differentiable rendering. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:28.640
Well, simple, we take a photograph, and have&nbsp;
an AI find a photorealistic material model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:35.440
that we can put into our light simulation program&nbsp;
that matches it. What this means is that now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.920
we can essentially put this real material&nbsp;
into a virtual world. This earlier work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.480
did very well with materials, but&nbsp;
it did not capture the geometry.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.120
Later, this followup work was looking to&nbsp;
improve the geometry side of things. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:01.360
let’s try to reproduce a 3D model from a&nbsp;
bunch of triangles. Let’s see….and. Look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:09.040
This is lovely. Seeing these images gradually&nbsp;
morph into the right solution is an absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.400
beautiful sight. But, of course, in&nbsp;
this case, the materials are gone.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.880
And, if we wish to focus more on materials,&nbsp;
and we don’t care about geometry at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:27.680
we can use one of our previous papers for that.&nbsp;
This one uses a learning algorithm to find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.760
about your artistic vision and&nbsp;
recommends really cool materials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:41.920
Or, with this other work, you can whip up a fake&nbsp;
material in photoshop, and it will magically find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:50.320
a digital photorealistic material that matches&nbsp;
it. But once again, no geometry. Materials only.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.720
But wait a minute. Am I reading this&nbsp;
right? With previous method number one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:04.480
we get the materials, but no geometry.
Or, previous method number two. No materials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:11.040
But, the geometry is much more detailed.
Or, previous method number three. Excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:17.040
materials, no geometry. We&nbsp;
can’t have it all, can we?

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:24.080
So, is that it? We can’t really take a real object&nbsp;
and make a virtual copy of it? Is the dream dead?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:31.280
Well, don’t despair quite yet! This new technique&nbsp;
might just be what we are looking for. Although I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:38.640
am not so sure, this problem is super challenging.&nbsp;
Let’s try this chair and see what happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.280
Here are a bunch of images of it.&nbsp;
Now, your turn, little algorithm!

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:52.200
And, we start out from a sphere. Well, I will&nbsp;
believe this when I see it! Good luck! And, hmm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.280
The shape is starting to change,&nbsp;
and…wait! Are you seeing what I am seeing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.560
Yes! Yes! Yes! The material is also&nbsp;
slowly starting to change. We are getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:13.440
not only geometry, but materials too! Oh boy!&nbsp;
Now, based on the previous works, our expectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:20.320
is that the result will be extremely coarse.&nbsp;
Well, hold on to your papers, and let’s see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:28.640
how much detail we end up with. Oh my. That is&nbsp;
so much better. Loving it! Actually, let’s check.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.040
This was the previous technique, and&nbsp;
this is the new one. My goodness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.440
I have to say there is no contest here. The&nbsp;
new one is so much more detailed! So good!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:48.960
And, it works on a bunch of other examples&nbsp;
too! These are not perfect by any means, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.640
this kind of improvement, just one more&nbsp;
paper down the line. That is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.840
incredible. The pace of progress in computer&nbsp;
graphics research is nothing short of amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:06.240
especially when we look at all the magic&nbsp;
scientists are doing in Wenzel Jakob’s lab.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:13.280
And, the whole process took less than an hour.&nbsp;
That is a considerable amount of time, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.920
an artist can leave a bunch of these&nbsp;
computations to cook overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.920
and by the morning, a bunch of already pretty&nbsp;
good quality virtual objects will appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:30.640
that we can place in our virtual worlds.&nbsp;
How cool is that? What a time to be alive!

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:36.400
Make sure to check out the whole paper,&nbsp;
it has crystal-clear mathematics, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.680
tons of gorgeous images. The link is&nbsp;
available in the video description. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.520
believe this will become an excellent tool&nbsp;
in democratizing asset creation for games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.120
animation movies, and all kinds of virtual worlds.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.280
So, what would you use this for? Do you have some&nbsp;
cool ideas? Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

